{tally_rows()}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="block-intro">High means lost stock, lost money, wrong orders or missed integrations in ordinary use. Medium needs unusual timing, input or a specific setting, or has a bounded effect. Low is noise or a wrong figure that corrects itself.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the problems have in common</h2>
    <ul class="patterns">
      <li><strong>The same action arriving twice is not handled.</strong> Finalizing, shipping, approving, cancelling and confirming all check the order's state before taking a lock, so two requests at the same moment both go through. This covers two staff members on one order, two browser tabs, and an integration that retries. Against a four-worker server these reproduced on every attempt.</li>
      <li><strong>IDs in a request are not checked against each other.</strong> Fulfillment and return calls accept lines of a different order, and with that they sidestep per-channel staff restrictions.</li>
      <li><strong>Order status is not checked where it matters.</strong> Cancelled and draft orders can be shipped and returned; a returned shipment can be cancelled.</li>
      <li><strong>Stock bookkeeping has gaps the database does not catch.</strong> Four uniqueness rules exist in code but not in the database, one reservation setting makes overselling possible, and stock notifications can name the wrong product.</li>
      <li><strong>Scheduled jobs carry plain calculation and filter mistakes.</strong> Order expiry miscounts days, and one voucher cleanup deletes records far beyond its target.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>All problems, most severe first</h2>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Problem</th><th>Severity</th><th>What it takes</th></tr></thead>
